Instruction: Read the excerpt from the Acquisition Bid Evaluation Agreement and answer the multiple-choice question by choosing the option that best characterizes the agreement.
Question: Is the 'financial point of view' the sole factor considered when determining whether an acquisition bid is superior?
Option A: No
Option B: Yes

Acquisition Bid Evaluation Agreement: 
SECTION 8.9. Evaluation of Bids. Notwithstanding any contrary provisions, at any point prior to the final bid submission, the Bid Review Committee (BRC) may determine that an Acquisition Bid qualifies as superior if, and only if, the following conditions are satisfied:
(i) the BRC, after extensive consultation with both its independent legal counsel and a third-party market strategist, confirms that the financial metrics provided, though crucial, are complemented by competitive positioning data;
(ii) in instances where the Bid includes a significant non-monetary component such as operational synergies or strategic alignment with long-term corporate goals, the BRC must also conduct an enhanced due diligence review that transcends standard financial analysis;
(iii) the BRC is required to compare the bid against alternative offers using a benchmark that integrates both fiscal evaluation and qualitative strategic insights;
(iv) any negotiation adjustments suggested by primary shareholders are required to be incorporated into a comprehensive assessment, ensuring that both monetary and non-monetary elements are given due weight.

SECTION 10.3. Superior Bid Determination. Even if a bid demonstrates outstanding fiscal projections, it will not be classified as superior exclusively on the basis of its financial merits if it fails to satisfy the qualitative benchmarks established above. All factors, including advisory inputs, strategic fit, and non-monetary synergies, must be fully considered.
